        "id": 1001696
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-14T16:58:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You may want to consider pre-computing these steps, it will simplify the code greatly and save time (and RAM probably). If I understand correctly, we have white-\u003ered (and back) and white-\u003egreen (and back). Reverse direction can use the same table in reverse, so we just need 2 tables?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 324,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 330,
        "endChar": 3
      },
      "revId": "0643749a0ae43934745d05b4812568944b1deab3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "327b3741_526bb3a4",
        "filename": "board/eve/led.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 330,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-14T19:06:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "white \u003c--\u003e red and white \u003c--\u003e green transitions are using the pre-computed table. The dynamic table handles all other transitions. Off \u003c--\u003e Red, Off \u003c--\u003e Green, Red \u003c--\u003e Red_2_3, and Red \u003c--\u003e Red_1_3. I was trying to avoid adding more tables and to avoid the logic for choosing which table to use. Right now, the only thing I have to check for is if new|old \u003d\u003d white. I would need to add 5 more tables to cover all cases. 0 -\u003e 20, 0 -\u003e 40, 0 -\u003e 80, 20 -\u003e 80, and 40 -\u003e 80. The other advantage is that this loop works regardless of what levels are chosen for the different colors.",
      "parentUuid": "f9dbd602_32a173a4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 324,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 330,
        "endChar": 3
      },
      "revId": "0643749a0ae43934745d05b4812568944b1deab3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
